{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 1230,
    "dDurationMs": 4430,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Most everyday phenomena happen equivalently\nin a mirror as they do normally - at least,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5660,
    "dDurationMs": 1320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "from a physics perspective."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 6980,
    "dDurationMs": 3370,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Unlike when you play a video backwards in\ntime (where it’s pretty obvious that something"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 10350,
    "dDurationMs": 4429,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "weird is going on), when compared with a normal\nvideo, motion in a mirrored video still looks"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 14779,
    "dDurationMs": 2681,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "totally physically normal - just mirrored."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 17460,
    "dDurationMs": 3809,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In fact, without outside context, there’s\nno way to tell which was the original and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 21269,
    "dDurationMs": 1141,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which was mirrored!"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 22410,
    "dDurationMs": 3619,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Which is why horizontally flopped shots are\nused in movies all the time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 26029,
    "dDurationMs": 3961,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In fact, as far as we know, everything in\nthe universe governed by electromagnetism"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 29990,
    "dDurationMs": 4370,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and gravity and the strong nuclear force behaves\nthis way - if you set up two experiments that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 34360,
    "dDurationMs": 3890,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are mirror images of one another, they’ll\nproduce results that look like mirror images"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 38250,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of each other."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 39250,
    "dDurationMs": 4520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Which presents a problem if we ever need to\ncommunicate with aliens from afar: if all"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 43770,
    "dDurationMs": 4339,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "physics is mirror-symmetric, that would mean\nleft handed and right handed are relative"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 48109,
    "dDurationMs": 2140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- from a physics perspective they’re interchangeable,\njust like up and down and forward and back"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 50249,
    "dDurationMs": 3601,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- so if we were simply talking to aliens and\ndidn’t have any shared reference objects,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 53850,
    "dDurationMs": 4299,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we’d we have no way of explaining what we\nmean by left-handed and right-handed using"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 58149,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "physics."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 59149,
    "dDurationMs": 3141,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": ". This Left/Right ambiguity is called “the\nOzma Problem.”"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 62290,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 63290,
    "dDurationMs": 3210,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And the distinction between left and right\nIS important, because earth-based life mostly"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 66500,
    "dDurationMs": 4360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "relies on sugars with right-handed symmetry\nand amino acids with left-handed symmetry."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 70860,
    "dDurationMs": 3000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This isn’t a physics constraint - it just\nas easily could have been the other way around"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 73860,
    "dDurationMs": 4780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- but the point is, the molecules in our food\nand our bodies DO have a specific orientation,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 78640,
    "dDurationMs": 4050,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "so not knowing left from right could impair\nintergalactic culinary relations."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 82690,
    "dDurationMs": 7380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "However, there is a solution: the weak nuclear\nforce doesn’t always play nicely when mirrored"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 90070,
    "dDurationMs": 5240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": ". For example, when uranium nuclei beta decay\nthey emit (mostly?) electrons spinning like"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 95310,
    "dDurationMs": 3720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "left-handed corkscrews, but if you perform\nthe mirror image of the experiment using a"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 99030,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "mirror-image uranium nucleus, the nucleus\nstill emits electrons spinning like left-handed"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 103990,
    "dDurationMs": 3820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "corkscrews (rather than right-handed, as they\nwould in a mirror) . It turns out that in"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 107810,
    "dDurationMs": 4390,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "our universe, the mirror-image of a physical\nprocess doesn’t always result in the mirror"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 112200,
    "dDurationMs": 4330,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "image of the outcome - uranium always decays\nmore into left-handed electrons, no matter"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 116530,
    "dDurationMs": 1120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "how you look at it."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 117650,
    "dDurationMs": 3620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So we’d tell the aliens “you know how\nelectrons spin when uranium decays?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 121270,
    "dDurationMs": 2190,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That direction is what we call ‘left’”."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 123460,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Which would solve the Ozma problem - except,\nthere’s a problem with this solution."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 129380,
    "dDurationMs": 2829,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Because what if the distant aliens were made\nentirely of antimatter?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 132209,
    "dDurationMs": 2810,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I mean, in principle they could be and we\nwouldn’t know."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 135019,
    "dDurationMs": 4461,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Antimatter interacting with itself behaves\nexactly like matter interacting with itself:"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 139480,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "antihydrogen has the same atomic spectrum\nas hydrogen, and antimatter-you looks and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 143560,
    "dDurationMs": 3420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "behaves exactly like matter-you (until it\ninteracts with matter)."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 146980,
    "dDurationMs": 4700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And here’s the problem: while the matter\nversion of a uranium nucleus decays into left-handed"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 151680,
    "dDurationMs": 4199,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "electrons whether it's in a mirror or not,\nthe anti-matter version always decays into…"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 155879,
    "dDurationMs": 3500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "right-handed anti-electrons whether it's in\na mirror or not."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 159379,
    "dDurationMs": 4241,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So if we told the aliens “look at the beta\ndecay of the nucleus with atomic weight 239"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 163620,
    "dDurationMs": 4110,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- that’s always the orientation we call\nleft-handed” we’d be wrong: for aliens"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 167730,
    "dDurationMs": 3110,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "made of antimatter, it would in fact be what\nwe call right handed."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 170840,
    "dDurationMs": 3250,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And you definitely don’t want to shake either\nhand of an alien made of antimatter."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 174090,
    "dDurationMs": 4099,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So how can you figure out, from afar, if a\ndistant alien is made of antimatter?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 178189,
    "dDurationMs": 2360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is the Ozma problem, level 2."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 180549,
    "dDurationMs": 3671,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Essentially, antimatter is another kind of\nmirror we can hold up to the universe, which"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 184220,
    "dDurationMs": 4219,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "combined with the possibility of regular mirroring\nmeans we can’t use beta decay to define"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 188439,
    "dDurationMs": 1541,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "left vs right."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 189980,
    "dDurationMs": 4509,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But luckily, there’s a next level solution,\nagain thanks to the weak nuclear force."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 194489,
    "dDurationMs": 3071,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Enter the Kaon, a fast-decaying subatomic\nparticle."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 197560,
    "dDurationMs": 4980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Whether they’re mirrored or not, around\n20.3% of the time Kaons decay into right-handed"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 202540,
    "dDurationMs": 5410,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "anti-electrons , while around 20.1% of the\ntime - slightly less often - they decay into"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 207950,
    "dDurationMs": 1989,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "left-handed electrons."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 209939,
    "dDurationMs": 4950,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And the key is this : if you instead take\nantimatter Kaons, whether mirrored or not,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 214889,
    "dDurationMs": 4440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "they still decay slightly less often into\nleft-handed electrons, rather than right-handed"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 219329,
    "dDurationMs": 2071,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "anti-electrons as you might have expected\nfrom the way antimatter-uranium decays."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 221400,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That is, normal kaons - whether mirrored or\nnot - and anti-kaons - whether mirrored or"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 226440,
    "dDurationMs": 3600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "not - both decay less often into left-handed\nelectrons."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 230040,
    "dDurationMs": 3809,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And this is how distant aliens could figure\nout if they’re made of matter or antimatter"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 233849,
    "dDurationMs": 4021,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and whether or not they’re using the same\nconcept of “left” as us: simply build"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 237870,
    "dDurationMs": 4199,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a particle accelerator and look at the decays\nof neutral kaon particles; the electron-like"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 242069,
    "dDurationMs": 4801,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "thing that they decay into slightly less frequently\nis made of what we call matter, and it’ll"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 246870,
    "dDurationMs": 2819,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "be moving in what we call a left-handed way."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 249689,
    "dDurationMs": 4491,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The universe doesn’t distinguish between\nleft and right or antimatter for electromagnetism,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 254180,
    "dDurationMs": 2350,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for gravity, and for the strong nuclear force."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 256530,
    "dDurationMs": 4750,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But for some reason, the weak force allows\nus to tell the difference."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 261280,
    "dDurationMs": 4070,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This video was made with the generous support\nof the Heising Simons foundation, which also"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 265350,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "supports research into the violation of antimatter-mirror\nsymmetry in our universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 269910,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Processes that violate antimatter-mirror symmetry\n(which is called “CP Violation”) are necessary"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 274230,
    "dDurationMs": 3770,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to explain why there’s so much more matter\nin universe than antimatter, and while the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 278000,
    "dDurationMs": 4070,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "weak nuclear force does violate that symmetry,\nit doesn’t come anywhere close to accounting"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 282070,
    "dDurationMs": 3340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for the observed imbalance between matter\nand antimatter in the universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 285410,
    "dDurationMs": 3560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So physicists around the world (among them\nresearchers supported by the Heising Simons"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 288970,
    "dDurationMs": 3980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "foundation) are searching for other possible\nprocesses that might break antimatter-mirror"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 292950,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "symmetry to help explain how the universe\nended up made mostly of matter, and thus,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 297190,
    "dDurationMs": 2120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "why it was possible for us to exist."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 299310,
    "dDurationMs": 1580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Thanks Heising Simons!"
    } ]
  } ]
}
